WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:09.720
Content-Type tells clients what kind of content is being served, such as HTML, JSON, JavaScript, CSS, images, or file downloads.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.540
Browsers use this information when deciding how to process a response.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.200
If the declared type is missing, wrong, or ambiguous, browsers may try to infer the type.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:26.860
That behavior is called MIME sniffing, and in some cases it can cause content to be interpreted more dangerously than the server intended.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:35.640
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff tells supporting browsers not to override certain declared content types, especially for scripts and styles.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:41.700
This helps reduce cases where a response served with an unexpected type is still treated as executable content.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:52.960
It is a small header, but it supports a larger discipline: serve content with accurate types, avoid ambiguous responses, and keep untrusted content away from executable contexts.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.380
File downloads and uploads need special care.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:04.360
User-supplied files should not be served from the same trusted origin in ways that allow them to execute as scripts or render as active HTML.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:09.940
Content-Disposition can help direct browsers to treat content as an attachment when appropriate.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:20.160
Storage names, content types, scanning, isolation, and download behavior should be designed together rather than relying on a file extension or a user-provided MIME type.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.660
MIME controls do not replace content validation.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:30.120
A malicious file can have a harmless-looking extension, and a harmless file can be mislabeled by a client.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:36.420
The server should set accurate response headers based on trusted handling, not blindly echo client-supplied metadata.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:44.960
For static assets, APIs, and uploads, predictable Content-Type behavior makes browser processing safer and troubleshooting easier.
